## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem assesses mastery of graph algorithms on DAGs, including topological sorting, feasibility verification under ordered constraints, and shortest-path computation.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort for DAGs
- DAG shortest-path DP (O(|V| + |E|))
- Feasibility checking with ordered mandatory edges
- Path reconstruction via predecessor pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestConstrainedPath(G, S, T, M) {
  const topo = topoSort(G);
  const idx = (v) => topo.indexOf(v);

  const canReach = (from, to) => {
    if (idx(from) >= idx(to)) return false;
    const reachable = new Set([from]);
    for (let i = idx(from); i < idx(to); i++) {
      for (const edge of G.adj[topo[i]]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(to)) reachable.add(edge.to);
      }
    }
    return reachable.has(to);
  };

  const points = [S, ...M.map(e => e[0]), ...M.map(e => e[1]), T];
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!canReach(points[i], points[i + 1])) return null;
  }

  const segmentCost = (src, dst) => {
    const dist = {}; dist[src] = 0;
    for (let i = idx(src); i < idx(dst); i++) {
      const u = topo[i];
      for (const edge of G.adj[u]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(dst)) {
          if (dist[u] + edge.w < (dist[edge.to] ?? Infinity)) {
            dist[edge.to] = dist[u] + edge.w;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return dist[dst];
  };

  let total = 0;
  const fullPath = [S];
  for (const [u, v, w] of M) {
    total += w;
    const seg = segmentCost(fullPath[fullPath.length - 1], u);
    if (seg === undefined) return null;
    total += seg;
  }
  const finalSeg = segmentCost(M[M.length - 1][1], T);
  if (finalSeg === undefined) return null;
  return total + finalSeg;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioned sequences with a non-additive batch cost and compares memoization vs tabulation. It also probes edge-case reasoning when K bounds vary.

## Key Concepts
- DP over contiguous partitions
- Range max tracking
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(a, K){
  const N=a.length, INF=1e18;
  const Kcap=Math.min(K,N);
  const dp=Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=>Array(Kcap+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
    for(let k=1;k<=Math.min(i,Kcap);k++){
      let maxV=0;
      for(let j=i-1;j>=0;j--){
        maxV = Math.max(maxV, a[j]);
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + maxV);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][Kcap];
}
```

